WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.370 --> 00:00:04.841
Even heroes make terrible mistakes from time
to time: in fact, in some movies, they're

00:00:04.841 --> 00:00:06.000
hardly heroes at all.

00:00:06.190 --> 00:00:10.460
With that in mind, here's a look at some of
the more glaring good guy misfires in movie

00:00:10.460 --> 00:00:10.960
history.

00:00:11.640 --> 00:00:14.800
There’s one thing you have to give to Daniel
Hillard: he loves his kids.

00:00:15.240 --> 00:00:16.800
“I need to be with my children.

00:00:17.680 --> 00:00:18.440
And I’ll do anything.”

00:00:18.440 --> 00:00:22.400
Unfortunately, that didn’t make him a good
husband, and when his marriage broke up, the

00:00:22.400 --> 00:00:26.860
out of work actor lost custody of his children
until he could get steady work and a place

00:00:26.860 --> 00:00:27.360
to live.

00:00:27.480 --> 00:00:30.720
It’s a little harsh, but also, “job”
and “home” are a couple of pretty low

00:00:30.720 --> 00:00:34.320
bars to clear for someone who’s supposed
to be raising three children.

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:38.899
And yet, when Daniel’s life inevitably crumbles
around him, he decides that it’s a better

00:00:38.899 --> 00:00:43.780
idea to create a false identity, dupe his
ex-wife into hiring him as a nanny to his

00:00:43.780 --> 00:00:47.240
own children, and then try to sabotage her
new relationship.

00:00:47.240 --> 00:00:52.260
Whether or not you buy into Mrs. Doubtfire's
heartwarming charm, that is straight up sociopathic

00:00:52.260 --> 00:00:52.900
behavior.

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:57.700
At best, he’s a liar, and at worst, dragging
your children into a complicated web of lies

00:00:57.710 --> 00:01:02.030
and deception is the sort of thing that’s
going to require years of therapy before they

00:01:02.030 --> 00:01:03.480
can even begin to trust again.

00:01:03.620 --> 00:01:08.680
Daniel has decent enough goals, but he betrays
the trust of his entire family, and unquestionably

00:01:08.680 --> 00:01:11.080
proves that his ex-wife was right about everything.

00:01:11.460 --> 00:01:16.360
It's easy to forget that in 2002’s horror
classic The Ring, Rachel Keller was a journalist

00:01:16.360 --> 00:01:19.200
chasing a lead tied to the suspicious death
of her niece.

00:01:19.240 --> 00:01:23.620
It’s that misguided search for the juicy
story that leads her to Samara's deadly tape,

00:01:23.620 --> 00:01:28.260
and it's her tragic misunderstanding of the
situation that leads her to "help" the accursed

00:01:28.260 --> 00:01:28.760
child.

00:01:29.000 --> 00:01:30.940
Needless to say, that was a bad idea.

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:35.720
Not only does she unleash Samara’s spirit
and cause yet another death, she also makes

00:01:35.720 --> 00:01:37.420
multiple copies of the tape.

00:01:37.700 --> 00:01:42.160
Again, she’s got the noble goal of saving
her son, but with two tapes out there, she’s

00:01:42.160 --> 00:01:45.350
also condemning unknown numbers of people
to die in the future.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:46.350
And the worst part?

00:01:46.350 --> 00:01:48.300
She didn’t even get to file her story.

00:01:48.620 --> 00:01:52.800
If you’ve seen Avengers: Age of Ultron,
you already know that Bruce Banner and Tony

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:56.820
Stark’s attempt at creating a helpful artificial
intelligence wound up going bad.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:01.780
In an act of astonishing hubris, these two
geniuses put their new creation in charge

00:02:01.790 --> 00:02:04.030
of Tony’s gadget factory and S.H.I.E.L.D.

00:02:04.030 --> 00:02:08.560
itself, and then went to a party without bothering
to check and see if anything had gone wrong.

00:02:08.770 --> 00:02:13.300
The result was Ultron, a killer robot who
destroyed Sokovia in what was undoubtedly

00:02:13.319 --> 00:02:18.349
the Marvel Cinematic Universe’s most devastating
act of destruction… at least until Thanos

00:02:18.349 --> 00:02:18.860
showed up.

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:23.300
While Banner responded to the incident by
flying off to space and spending two years

00:02:23.310 --> 00:02:27.739
as a big green gladiator (hey, it was actually
cheaper than therapy), Tony just kept making

00:02:27.740 --> 00:02:28.560
things worse.

00:02:28.560 --> 00:02:33.220
His guilt over Sokovia led to the registration
act that prompted his Civil War with Captain

00:02:33.220 --> 00:02:38.360
America, which in turn left Earth’s heroes
divided when Thanos arrived with the Infinity

00:02:38.360 --> 00:02:38.860
Stones.

00:02:38.960 --> 00:02:43.200
Tony’s still out there trying to fix things,
but given his track record over the past few

00:02:43.200 --> 00:02:46.420
years, maybe he should just focus on making
a new phone.

00:02:46.700 --> 00:02:50.819
Stars Wars fans the world over would have
you believe that Obi-Wan Kenobi is one of

00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:55.320
the wisest characters in a galaxy far, far
away, but let’s be honest with each other.

00:02:55.650 --> 00:02:58.860
Obi-Wan pretty much messes up everything,
all the time.

00:02:58.870 --> 00:03:03.670
In Star Wars Episode 1: The Phantom Menace,
Young Obi-Wan almost single-handedly destroys

00:03:03.670 --> 00:03:08.099
the Jedi when he demands, against the better
judgement of Yoda and pretty much every member

00:03:08.100 --> 00:03:11.040
of the Jedi Council, to train Anakin Skywalker.

00:03:11.230 --> 00:03:15.300
Spoiler warning, but that didn’t work out
so well for him, or any of the younglings

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:18.920
at Jedi School, or the people of Alderaan,
or… well, you get the idea.

00:03:19.220 --> 00:03:22.800
Jump ahead to A New Hope, and you’ll see
Obi-Wan doesn't really fare much better by

00:03:22.800 --> 00:03:26.060
keeping the identity of Luke Skywalker's father
a secret.

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:30.380
If nothing else, knowing his full story from
the start of things could've saved him from

00:03:30.380 --> 00:03:32.300
passionately kissing his own sister.

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:38.420
Grandpa Joe is unquestionably set up as Charlie
Bucket's personal hero in 1971's Willy Wonka

00:03:38.420 --> 00:03:39.520
and the Chocolate Factory.

00:03:39.840 --> 00:03:44.200
He’s the first person Charlie runs to after
winning the contest, and he's also Charlie's

00:03:44.200 --> 00:03:48.580
plus-one on their fateful tour of the unhinged
candy tycoon's confection factory.

00:03:48.580 --> 00:03:52.460
Before we get into that psychedelic tour,
though, let's revisit the fact that Grandpa

00:03:52.469 --> 00:03:54.469
Joe is bedridden when the film begins.

00:03:54.469 --> 00:03:59.440
He even claims to be incapable of working,
thus leaving his family to live in utter poverty,

00:03:59.440 --> 00:04:03.620
until Charlie comes charging through the door
with a ticket to unlimited chocolate.

00:04:03.629 --> 00:04:08.329
The second that happens, Grandpa Joe is suddenly
and suspiciously capable of dancing around

00:04:08.329 --> 00:04:12.080
the one-room hovel where he’s been living
rent-free for who knows how long.

00:04:12.250 --> 00:04:16.760
On the tour, it's Joe who encourages Charlie
to to steal some of that fizzy-lifting drink,

00:04:16.769 --> 00:04:20.829
which nearly leads them both to being diced
up by the fan in the ceiling vent and almost

00:04:20.829 --> 00:04:23.990
disqualifies Charlie from inheriting Wonka's
factory.

00:04:23.990 --> 00:04:27.210
It’s always nice to spend some time with
your grandparents, but maybe Charlie would

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:30.980
be better off not hanging out with a geriatric,
kleptomaniac con man.

00:04:31.660 --> 00:04:36.160
On paper, Scott Pilgrim's tale is in fact
a romantic epic full of 8-bit flair.

00:04:36.420 --> 00:04:41.509
Edgar Wright's stylish blend of action, comedy
and romance makes Scott Pilgrim vs. the World

00:04:41.509 --> 00:04:46.420
a one-of-a-kind cinematic experience, so much
so that it's easy to forget that Scott himself

00:04:46.420 --> 00:04:47.520
is a bit of a scumbag.

00:04:47.740 --> 00:04:51.720
Sure, it's a bummer that he's forced to fight
and defeat all eight of Ramona Flowers' evil

00:04:51.729 --> 00:04:55.389
exes in order to prove his love, but let's
not forget that the twenty-something Scott

00:04:55.389 --> 00:04:59.530
was dating naïve high-schooler Knives Chau
the whole time he was pursuing Ramona.

00:04:59.530 --> 00:05:04.310
Though Scott atones for his actions late in
the film, he never really does right by Knives,

00:05:04.310 --> 00:05:08.760
ultimately leaving her broken-hearted and
treading water in his oh-so romantic, self-serving

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:09.260
wake.

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:13.820
In Shaun of the Dead, Simon Pegg might just
be playing the ultimate slacker hero.

00:05:13.830 --> 00:05:18.580
He works at a low-level electronics store,
he can't remember his mother's birthday, he

00:05:18.580 --> 00:05:22.460
hangs out with his weed-dealing bestie way
too much, and he can't seem to commit to his

00:05:22.460 --> 00:05:23.960
longtime girlfriend Liz.

00:05:23.960 --> 00:05:28.692
When the dead walk and threaten to end humanity,
Shaun finally steps up and sets out to save

00:05:28.692 --> 00:05:33.199
the lives of everyone he loves by taking them
to his favorite pub and having a drink while

00:05:33.200 --> 00:05:34.140
things blow over.

00:05:34.440 --> 00:05:38.710
Day drinking during the apocalypse might sound
like a good idea, but in Shaun’s case, it’s

00:05:38.710 --> 00:05:40.100
an absolute disaster.

00:05:40.100 --> 00:05:44.340
Even setting aside the perilous road to get
to the Winchester, matters get out of hand

00:05:44.340 --> 00:05:48.540
quickly once Shaun and his crew find their
way inside and people start to die.

00:05:48.540 --> 00:05:49.540
Not Shaun, though.

00:05:49.640 --> 00:05:53.200
He somehow finds a way to safety, and even
manages to save Liz.

00:05:53.200 --> 00:05:56.580
Still, Shaun will be forced to live the rest
of his life with the blood of those fallen

00:05:56.580 --> 00:06:00.440
companions on his hands, giving a whole new
meaning to the phrase, "You've got red on

00:06:00.440 --> 00:06:00.940
you."

00:06:01.320 --> 00:06:04.860
Here's a few pointers for all of the lovers
out there who can't quite tell whether or

00:06:04.860 --> 00:06:07.800
not that certain someone you've just met is
"the one."

00:06:07.800 --> 00:06:11.470
If you wake up in the middle of the night
and find them in your room, uninvited and

00:06:11.470 --> 00:06:14.040
watching you sleep, it's time to end that
relationship.

00:06:14.250 --> 00:06:17.740
If they break up with you in the middle of
the woods and leave you there crying and alone

00:06:17.760 --> 00:06:20.780
rather than giving you a ride home, you should
stay broken up.

00:06:21.080 --> 00:06:24.530
If they're overprotective to the point of
threatening your friends just because they're

00:06:24.530 --> 00:06:27.220
talking to you, you might want to take some
space.

00:06:27.530 --> 00:06:31.280
If they ever almost kill you because they
really, really like the taste of your blood,

00:06:31.280 --> 00:06:33.140
you should probably seek out the authorities.

00:06:33.320 --> 00:06:37.300
And for Pete's sake, if they turn to you after
just a few dates and declare, "You are my

00:06:37.300 --> 00:06:39.820
life now," just go ahead and get a restraining
order.

00:06:40.330 --> 00:06:44.960
Since Twilight hit theaters in 2008, some
of Edward Cullen’s sparkly sheen has lost

00:06:44.970 --> 00:06:49.550
its luster, and people are starting to see
that he was really just an undead creep, and

00:06:49.550 --> 00:06:53.100
that Bella's life would've been totally fine
if he had just left her alone.

00:06:53.780 --> 00:06:57.940
Being a superhero means making enemies, and
making enemies means the people you love will

00:06:57.949 --> 00:06:59.030
always be in peril.

00:06:59.030 --> 00:07:03.370
That's the one lesson Peter Parker learns
the hard way in The Amazing Spider-Man 2,

00:07:03.370 --> 00:07:06.610
and he learns it at the expense of his girlfriend
Gwen Stacy.

00:07:06.610 --> 00:07:10.530
Of course, Peter didn't have to learn that
lesson at all, because he had someone explicitly

00:07:10.530 --> 00:07:12.460
lay out the the stakes of being a hero.

00:07:12.580 --> 00:07:17.000
Gwen's father, Police Captain George Stacy,
died while saving Spider-Man's life.

00:07:17.009 --> 00:07:22.039
With his dying words, he had one simple request:
that Peter not date his daughter, so she'd

00:07:22.040 --> 00:07:24.340
never be the target of one of Spider-Man's
enemies.

00:07:24.640 --> 00:07:29.290
Alas, Peter completely ignores it in the sequel,
so it should come as no surprise that Gwen

00:07:29.290 --> 00:07:34.120
is eventually taken hostage by the bad guys,
and pays for Peter’s heroics with her life.

00:07:34.420 --> 00:07:37.200
If only someone had warned him this exact
thing would happen!

00:07:37.820 --> 00:07:38.400
Oh, wait.

